body:
@JAWS-test 
> I think summary and details are good for accordion. Especially since the name attribute combines the individual details into one element: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#attr-details-name

That's not accurate. The `name` does not combine `<details>` (with a matching `name` value) into one element. For now, all it does is enforce a rule that only one in a set of `<details>` with a matching `name` value can be open at a time.

The `name` does not even provide an overall grouping context nor accName for the associated `<details>` elements (dbaron already knows my thoughts on this, so not tagging).

@joshuaboele As Scott noted above (and [more succinctly over here](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8864#issuecomment-1428404856)), headings are allowed but UA implementations vary. Since this isn't a support forum you may want to ask for peoples' experience addressing this in a dedicated forum (social media with #a11y hashtag, A11y Slack, mailing lists, etc.).

@dev-nicolaos re:
>I worry making the disclosure triangle (::marker) the only thing to trigger the toggle behavior is going to be a pretty big hit to usability.

that's a solid concern - but there's also the solution of making the disclosure marker the button part in the shadow dom, and the rest of the summary still behave as a clickable label.  this sort of 'treat the summary as a label' idea was already alluded / mentioned by simon and myself in prior comments, though.

@js-choi That use case makes sense, although the concern with too small of a pointer target still remains. Maybe that's something browsers could partially address with user agent styles when developers opt-in to this paradigm?

> Apps that opt into Behavior B—at least outliner apps—would probably never hide the arrow marker (unless they provided a custom toggle button in a separate element). They would only opt in if they need toggle functionality and interactivity of summary content (e.g., editability) at the same time.

I would also like to think people wouldn't hide it, but I've seen poorer choices made on the web. That being said, I acknowledge almost every affordance of the web platform can become a foot gun (a lack of contrast between text and background colors for example), and we can't let "someone might misuse it" prevent us from ever moving the platform forward. Maybe the spec could include a non-normative note calling out this concern for developers.
